><highlight color="#9643FC" opacity="0.399994" creationdate="D:20250515121721+10'00'" flags="print" IT="HighlightNote" date="D:20250515121726+10'00'" name="ab9ea8ff-5508-4583-a63a-3aa4e42a3cc5" page="11" coords="200.012000,298.205000,565.514000,298.205000,200.012000,289.205000,565.514000,289.205000,200.012000,285.223000,567.592000,285.223000,200.012000,276.223000,567.592000,276.223000,200.012000,272.184000,562.867000,272.184000,200.012000,263.184000,562.867000,263.184000,200.012000,259.201000,571.774000,259.201000,200.012000,250.201000,571.774000,250.201000,200.012000,246.218000,564.969000,246.218000,200.012000,237.218000,564.969000,237.218000,200.012000,233.179000,569.845000,233.179000,200.012000,224.179000,569.845000,224.179000,200.012000,220.197000,562.214000,220.197000,200.012000,211.197000,562.214000,211.197000,200.012000,207.214000,574.281000,207.214000,200.012000,198.214000,574.281000,198.214000,200.012000,194.175000,575.601000,194.175000,200.012000,185.175000,575.601000,185.175000,200.012000,181.192000,566.757000,181.192000,200.012000,172.192000,566.757000,172.192000,200.012000,168.209000,534.669000,168.209000,200.012000,159.209000,534.669000,159.209000,200.012000,155.227000,568.773000,155.227000,200.012000,146.227000,568.773000,146.227000,200.012000,142.188000,572.974000,142.188000,200.012000,133.188000,572.974000,133.188000,200.012000,129.205000,571.643000,129.205000,200.012000,120.205000,571.643000,120.205000,200.012000,116.222000,572.922000,116.222000,200.012000,107.222000,572.922000,107.222000,200.012000,103.183000,564.266000,103.183000,200.012000,94.183100,564.266000,94.183100,200.012000,90.200900,550.937000,90.200900,200.012000,81.201100,550.937000,81.201100" rect="199.731000,80.919800,575.882000,298.486000" subject="Comment on Text" title="alexd"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Our DTW based framework enables analysis and interpretation of complex trajectories over&#xD;many games and teams, and the generation of key summary metrics of play outcomes. Comparison&#xD;of trajectory clusters within and between countries can assist in identifying key patterns&#xD;to support coaching and preparation for games. A key component of this is the ability to&#xD;highlight key plays for targeted video review from a large database of game footage, assisting&#xD;end users with limited time resources. A common methodology used in current and previous&#xD;research utilises Euclidean distances [</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#2B5BFA;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>3</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>–</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#2B5BFA;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>5</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>] which does not consider data alignment and will&#xD;miss out similar plays when analysed under unsupervised conditions. Existing works that used&#xD;DTW in basketball tended to focus on player movements, and did not explicitly relate trajectories&#xD;to play outcomes with a focus on both spatial and temporal characteristic of the trajectories&#xD;[</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#2B5BFA;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>15</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>,</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#2B5BFA;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>16</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>]. Our framework contributes a practical means to cluster spatio-temporal&#xD;trajectories and relate them to play outcomes, using ball movement data. However, the use of&#xD;k-means to cluster trajectory endpoints is limited in that it cannot take into account boundary&#xD;conditions associated with court boundaries. In addition, there is immense diversity in trajectories&#xD;and determining how many clusters to use is somewhat subjective; potentially, a combination&#xD;of using a vocabulary of discrete actions [</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#2B5BFA;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>16</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>] and a Diricihlet Process Mixture Model&#xD;[</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#2B5BFA;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>29</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>] could better cluster trajectories and inherently finds the optimal number of clusters.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="11" rect="612.000000,206.205000,796.000000,298.205000"
